ard to I7(empty)? No, that’s wrong direction.  \n\nActually: Diagonal from F10(O)–G9(O)–H8(O) is three in a row down-left.  \nContinuation: upward along that line is E11(empty)–F10(O)–G9(O)–H8(O)–I7(empty) — not yet four.  \n\nBut maybe immediate win:  \nCheck **vertical H7–H10** four O’s, blocked both ends, so no.  \nCheck **horizontal G10–H10–I10** only three O’s? No, G10(O), H10(O), I10(X) — only two O’s.  \n\nWait, let’s carefully recount occupied list:  \nH6=X, H7=O, H8=O, H9=O, H10=O, H11=X.  \n\nSo vertical O’s: H7, H8, H9, H10 — yes four in a row. But H6 and H11 are X, so no H5 or H12 possible for five. So vertical four is useless.  \n\nCheck diagonal: G9(O), H8(O), I7(empty) — not yet threat.  \n\nBut G11(O) and H10(O) and I9(empty) — not aligned.  \n\nActually, the most promising is **diagonal from G10(O) to D13**:  \nG10(O), H11(X) blocked.  \n\nBetter: Look at **diagonal from F10(O)–G9(O)–H8(O)**. Continue upward: E11(empty) and downward: I7(empty). So it’s an open three (E11 empty, I7 empty) if we place O at either E11 or I7, but only one of them makes a four? Let’s check:  \nIf I play O at **E11**, then E11–F10–G9–H8 is four in a row diagonal down-left. Then Black must block at D12 or I7. But if they block one end, I still have the other end to make five next turn? Let’s see: after E11(O), F10(O), G9(O), H8(O), I7(empty) is open for next move to win if Black doesn’t block both ends. But Black can only block one in one turn, so that’s a winning threat — open four.  \n\nBut wait, is E11–F10–G9–H8 really four in a row? Yes, diagonal direction: row decreases by 1, col increases by 1 from E11 to H8. Check: E11(empty), F10(O), G9(O), H8(O) — if I play E11(O), that’s four consecutive stones diagonal. Then two open ends: D12(empty) and I7(empty). That’s an *open four* — Black cannot stop both in one move, so White wins next turn.  \n\nCheck legality: E11 is not in occupied list, so valid.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nThe move E11 creates an open four in the diagonal (d